WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:06.240
"Haikyuu!!" set the bar as one of the most popular&nbsp;
manga, turned anime, turned stage play. But who is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:11.220
the mysterious author behind the series? And what&nbsp;
does that foot mean on all the boys' uniforms?

00:00:11.935 --> 00:00:16.020
The big goal for the Karasuno volleyball team&nbsp;
in "Haikyuu!!" is to win a tournament known as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:20.280
the "Inter-high." When the team plays in the&nbsp;
Spring Inter-High in Tokyo for the first time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:24.480
they are amazed by the scale of the event&nbsp;
— the huge arenas, the number of courts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:29.520
the number of teams, and the TV filming crews&nbsp;
are all beyond expectations. This is a real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:34.101
tournament in Japan, and they are just as big of&nbsp;
a deal in real life as they are in "Haikyuu!!"

00:00:34.101 --> 00:00:37.139
"It's so much cooler to see it in person, man!"

00:00:37.139 --> 00:00:39.180
When the Inter-highs were canceled in 2020,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:43.260
the team behind "Haikyuu!!" put together&nbsp;
a project in support of the students that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:48.240
wouldn't get to play that year. The staff&nbsp;
collected 165 uniforms of real teams that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:52.260
had been hoping to play in the tournament and&nbsp;
arranged them in a special commemorative photo.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:57.180
For third-year students who would graduate in&nbsp;
2020, the cancellation meant that they lost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:01.380
their final opportunity to play in a high school&nbsp;
tournament, which can be quite disheartening.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:05.839
The photo included a message to the players&nbsp;
which can be translated into something like:

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:14.400
The official "Haikyuu!!" website also&nbsp;
publishes free posters using anime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.720
and manga art which school volleyball clubs&nbsp;
can use to attract and recruit new members.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:23.700
School sports clubs, or "Bukatsu," are&nbsp;
a big deal in Japan, and the majority of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:28.680
students take part in a club from the age of&nbsp;
12 until they graduate high school at 18. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:32.700
"Haikyuu!!," Karasuno team members are seen&nbsp;
turning up at the gym before school begins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:37.620
and staying after school past sundown. They attend&nbsp;
practice matches and tournaments on weekends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:41.640
and club practice is still held around three&nbsp;
times a week during the summer holidays.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:45.000
Because of all this, it can be easy to&nbsp;
think that the protagonist and his team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.320
are going above and beyond the usual, but&nbsp;
in actuality, all of these activities are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:53.340
standard practice in the world of Bukatsu.&nbsp;
It's an extreme commitment for the kids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:57.300
their parents, and even the teachers, who&nbsp;
often need to look after the clubs during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:01.740
the summer and outside of work hours, but&nbsp;
may not even get any extra pay for it.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:04.140
"That would be ridiculously ridiculous."

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:08.880
Unsurprisingly, club activities can also take&nbsp;
up time students would otherwise spend studying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:11.520
which can lead to some conflicts of interest. Just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:14.460
like the exam troubles Hinata&nbsp;
has at the start of Season 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:18.900
real students can also miss out on important club&nbsp;
outings if their grades are not up to scratch.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:25.380
Collaboration cafes are a standard superfan&nbsp;
activity in Japan, and many popular franchises&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:30.600
such as "Final Fantasy 14" or "Kirby" have&nbsp;
permanent cafes in big cities. For anime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:33.540
however, it's more common to&nbsp;
see limited-time "pop-up" cafes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:37.800
often when new content for the franchise&nbsp;
is released. For instance, when "Haikyuu!!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:42.540
To The Top" was airing, a limited-time&nbsp;
"Animate X Haikyuu!!" cafe opened in Tokyo.

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:46.980
The cafe provided a place for fans to sit&nbsp;
and eat themed food while surrounded by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:51.540
artwork of their favorite characters, and even&nbsp;
be served by waiters in character cosplay. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:55.980
drinks were themed after different Karasuno&nbsp;
team members, such as Hinata's Orange Drink,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.980 --> 00:03:00.900
or Kageyama's Concentration Cocoa, and&nbsp;
each menu item came with a special coaster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:05.640
with original character art. Most themed&nbsp;
cafes also serve as special pop-up shops,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:10.800
which sell limited-edition merchandise, often&nbsp;
with special art drawn especially for the cafe.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:15.540
Multiple franchises can sometimes join&nbsp;
forces to present special cafes too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:20.460
and in 2022, "Haikyuu!!" collaborated with&nbsp;
the popular video game "Monster Hunter:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:26.520
Rise" for a special Capcom cafe. These cafes&nbsp;
can be especially popular, as fans get to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:31.099
their favorite characters in outfits and contexts&nbsp;
that would never exist in their own universes.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:34.500
As odd as it may seem, stage&nbsp;
plays are pretty common in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:37.860
world of anime and manga. Once&nbsp;
a manga has an anime adaptation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:43.260
the next step is often a live-action drama or&nbsp;
a theater production. Many popular franchises,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:48.180
such as "Naruto" and "Tokyo Ghoul," have produced&nbsp;
stage plays, and "Haikyuu!!" is no exception.

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:54.540
The "Hyper Projection Play: Haikyuu!!" stage&nbsp;
performance ran in 2015 and 2016 and used a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:59.040
mixture of manga art, video animation,&nbsp;
and live performance to tell the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:03.300
of the formation of the Karasuno team. The&nbsp;
play involves a lot of dancing and singing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:08.940
unlike the original material, but still has plenty&nbsp;
of "Haikyuu"-style goofy comedy. Merchandise for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:13.020
the show was also made, which can still be&nbsp;
found on certain sites even in the West.

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:18.180
Although the play is in Japanese, performances&nbsp;
held in Tokyo offered "subtitle glasses," which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:22.560
displayed English subtitles for the show&nbsp;
when worn. A DVD of the performance was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:27.060
also released in 2015, which included bonus&nbsp;
features such as interviews with the cast.

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:31.620
The play is a completely standalone project&nbsp;
and does not involve any of the voice actors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:35.940
from the anime, but the world of anime&nbsp;
theater has its own stars. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:39.540
the actor playing Kageyama also starred&nbsp;
in the "Prince of Tennis" musical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:44.280
and both the Hinata and Kageyama actors&nbsp;
starred in a "Naruto" stage play as well.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:49.020
Despite penning an award-winning manga&nbsp;
with a successful anime adaptation and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:54.660
a loyal fan following, the author known as&nbsp;
Haruichi Furudate is a mystery. The popularity of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:59.820
"Haikyuu!!" has continued for over a decade now,&nbsp;
and yet Haruichi's face has never been revealed.

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:03.240
Since "Haikyuu!!" is a "shonen" manga,&nbsp;
meaning it is aimed at young boys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:08.400
it is often assumed that Haruichi is male, and&nbsp;
this is reflected in Viz Media's decision to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:12.060
use male pronouns in their official&nbsp;
translation of the manga. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:14.940
Haruichi's gender has never&nbsp;
been officially confirmed.

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:17.700
Pronouns don't need to be used&nbsp;
at all in the Japanese language,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:23.160
so Haruichi is simply referred to by name&nbsp;
with no gender-specific language. Other kinds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:27.900
of information on the author are also scarce, so&nbsp;
it's not really possible to infer gender that way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:33.000
either. Though Haruichi is a male name, it could&nbsp;
easily be a pen name. And while we know that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:37.200
played volleyball in junior high school, girls'&nbsp;
volleyball clubs are just as common as boys'.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:41.220
When Haruichi's comments or interviews are&nbsp;
published on the "Shonen JUMP" website,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:46.140
the icon used is a strange bird character&nbsp;
wearing some sort of suit. Additionally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:51.120
some fans in Japan believe that "Haikyuu!!" is&nbsp;
written from a very female perspective, despite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:55.468
being published in a magazine for young boys —&nbsp;
and they suspect that the author could be female.

00:05:55.578 --> 00:05:57.678
"Does it uh, even really matter?"

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:02.100
In Japan, the "Haikyuu!!" manga was&nbsp;
serialized in the Weekly Shonen Jump magazine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:07.800
alongside other big names such as "One Piece,"&nbsp;
"Naruto," "Hunter X Hunter," and many more. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:12.840
magazine began in 1968 and is an iconic&nbsp;
part of modern Japanese manga culture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:16.140
which naturally means that Shonen&nbsp;
Jump merchandise stores exist.

00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:19.620
Across Japan, there are 12 of these&nbsp;
JUMP Shops in cities such as Tokyo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:24.900
Osaka, Nagoya, Hiroshima, and Fukuoka.&nbsp;
"Haikyuu!!" remains one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:29.100
popular series that Jump owns — despite&nbsp;
it being finished — which means that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:32.880
JUMP Shops devote a good portion of their&nbsp;
display space to the "Haikyuu!!" franchise.

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:38.760
Common merchandise types include stationery,&nbsp;
acrylic keyrings, acrylic stands, lip balms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:44.520
and accessories, phone cases, card cases, and&nbsp;
more. Merchandise for a show with many characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:47.820
like "Haikyuu!!" is often categorized&nbsp;
and displayed by teams and characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:53.460
so fans can find their favorite quickly and&nbsp;
easily. The JUMP shops also sell special&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:57.798
birthday badges of "Haikyuu!!" characters&nbsp;
each year when their birthday comes around.

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:04.440
The Karasuno team members Yu Nishinoya&nbsp;
and Asahi Azumane serve as foils,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:08.520
intended by Furudate to compare and&nbsp;
contrast against one another. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:12.600
shown through mirroring elements of their&nbsp;
characters, such as their birthdays -– 10/10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:18.000
and 01/01. Additionally, Asahi is the&nbsp;
tallest member of the Karasuno team at 6' 1",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:22.800
and Nishinoya is the shortest at 5'&nbsp;
3" — a full inch shorter than Hinata.

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:25.140
"And Nishinoya is only about 150cm."

00:07:25.468 --> 00:07:27.944
"I'm 160!"

00:07:27.944 --> 00:07:32.820
Other notable contrasts between the two characters&nbsp;
include their personalities. Nishinoya is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.820 --> 00:07:37.140
boisterous and full of confidence, constantly&nbsp;
taking part in silly behavior with Tanaka.

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:37.709
"Ahh!"

00:07:37.709 --> 00:07:41.940
"Nice one. Ok, you can stop now."

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:47.460
In contrast, Asahi is quiet and slightly shy,&nbsp;
which leads to some confidence issues. Judging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:51.840
by these personalities alone, you might think&nbsp;
that Nishinoya was the front-and-center ace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:56.340
of the team and Asahi the behind-the-scenes&nbsp;
libero, but in actuality, it's the opposite.

00:07:56.340 --> 00:08:02.820
Kageyama and Oikawa are also seen as foils&nbsp;
due to the way Oikawa contrasts Kageyama's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:07.620
play style and interpersonal skills. Kageyama is&nbsp;
a brilliant setter because he has the physical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:13.020
ability to handle the ball with precision, but&nbsp;
he's bad at communicating. Oikawa, meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:18.000
doesn't have particularly precise skills, but he&nbsp;
scores points by communicating with his teammates.

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:23.520
Due to the fast-paced nature of the anime&nbsp;
industry, clearing trademarks for use isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:28.740
considered a top priority, and most shows cut&nbsp;
out this part of the process entirely. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:34.020
names like McDonald's, Apple, Starbucks, and&nbsp;
Google undergo slight spelling or thematic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.020 --> 00:08:39.780
changes that result in the birth of gems&nbsp;
like Wacdnalds, Pear, Starducks, and Zoogle.

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:43.740
"Haikyuu!!" is no different in this regard,&nbsp;
and you may have spotted convenience stores&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:48.900
named 8/11 and electronics by Somy hiding in&nbsp;
the show. However, have you noticed the fake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:53.100
brand name on the Karasuno uniform before?&nbsp;
If you look closely you'll notice the team's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:57.780
uniforms all bear a logo consisting of a&nbsp;
foot and the letters "i-c-s." When read&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.780 --> 00:09:02.280
with the Japanese word for foot, this logo&nbsp;
spells "Asics" and is a parody of the famous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.280 --> 00:09:06.660
sports brand Asics which is especially&nbsp;
well-known for its volleyball products.

00:09:06.660 --> 00:09:11.580
This was an unconfirmed reference for a&nbsp;
while, but when Asics asked Furudate about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:16.080
they confirmed the parody, explaining that&nbsp;
they wore Asics shoes while playing volleyball&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:20.220
in school. Since then, the sports brand&nbsp;
has carried out campaigns with "Haikyu!!"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:24.720
featuring the art of characters with the&nbsp;
real Asics logo emblazoned on their chests.

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:29.820
Japan is a country full of language-based&nbsp;
easter eggs, puns, and hidden meanings. One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:33.660
of the biggest ones in "Haikyuu!!" is that its&nbsp;
title is an old term for volleyball in Japanese.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:38.340
The word is spelled with the Chinese character&nbsp;
for "throw" or "expel" and the character for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:42.600
"ball." Believe it or not, this can work as a&nbsp;
"Did you know?" fact even for native speakers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:47.820
as the word is now obscure enough that many young&nbsp;
Japanese people don't know it. Nowadays, the sport&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:52.500
is referred to as "Barebōru" instead, which is&nbsp;
simply volleyball written in Japanese characters.

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:57.300
Much of the language related to the sport&nbsp;
is actually derived from English. Spikes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.300 --> 00:10:00.540
and serves are still known by the same&nbsp;
name in Japanese, and you'll also find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.540 --> 00:10:05.100
the characters shouting English phrases during&nbsp;
matches. These include "Donmai," which simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:09.360
means "don't mind," and is used to show support&nbsp;
when someone drops the ball or makes a mistake.

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:15.366
Don't mind, Kageyama! Don't,&nbsp;
don't mind! Kageyama –"

00:10:15.366 --> 00:10:19.620
Next, "Haikyuu!!" fans have probably heard&nbsp;
characters shout "Naisukī," which means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.620 --> 00:10:23.382
"nice kill." Teammates say this to each other&nbsp;
when they hit a good spike and score a point.

00:10:23.382 --> 00:10:24.222
"Nice kill!"

00:10:25.834 --> 00:10:30.420
Another phrase commonly heard is "Naissā,"&nbsp;
which means "nice serve." This is often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.420 --> 00:10:33.720
shouted by a teammate when a player is&nbsp;
about to serve to cheer on other players,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:36.240
as serving is known to be&nbsp;
tough on players' nerves.

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:38.040
"Nice serve!"

00:10:38.040 --> 00:10:43.020
One funny thing to think about is that due to&nbsp;
their poor grades in subjects like English, Hinata&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.020 --> 00:10:47.460
and Kageyama probably don't know that they're&nbsp;
speaking English-based phrases in matches at all.

